  "fibres": [],
  "measures": [],
  "notes": "A half-wave plate turns a laser’s linear polarisation; a calcite Wollaston prism splits it into s and p, which leave at an angle to each other, each onto a photodiode. Press Play on the waveplate’s axis sweep: as the plate turns by θ the polarisation turns by 2θ, and the power moves from one diode to the other as cos² and sin². With the plate at 22.5° the two are balanced, which is how a balanced polarimeter reads small rotations."
